WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:05.640
RORY PETTY: Hi.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.340
And welcome to Alerting
and Cloud Monitoring.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.420
My name is Rory.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.850
And I'm a Product Manager
for Cloud Monitoring.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.850
And I'm joined by Syed, who
is a software engineer, who

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.830
also works on cloud monitoring.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.990
So today, we're going
to talk about how

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.230
you create alerts,
what types of metrics

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.210
are available to alert on.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.390
We'll delve a little bit into
the metric and resource model

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.070
in Cloud Monitoring.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.110
And we'll show a couple
of demos that present

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.330
different alerting concepts.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.090
So Cloud Monitoring is
part of the Cloud Operation

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.040
Suite at Google.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.490
Theis core infrastructure
powers not only Google Cloud,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.620
but all of internal Google.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.680
So you can build your operations
workflows with confidence

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.490
on top of our
Cloud Ops products.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.250
You might be familiar
with the term Stackdriver.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.380
That was previously the
name for the monitoring

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.000
product in Google Cloud.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.700
We've since rebranded it to
Google Cloud Monitoring just

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.570
to further emphasize that we
are the primary monitoring

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.510
and operations, suite of
tools, for all of Google Cloud.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:12.000
So you can find alerting by
going to the Hamburger menu

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.630
in the upper-left-hand
corner of the Cloud console,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.110
clicking it, scrolling down
to the Operations section,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.920
selecting Monitoring
and selecting Alerting.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.510
In alerting, you'll
find the ability

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.400
to create other policies.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.280
You'll see incidents that
have been created from alerts.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.560
And you'll also see
notification channels,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.860
which let you route the
alerts to the correct person

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:36.740
or system.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.380
So what is an Alert Policy?

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.290
Alert Policies let you
define a set of conditions

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.180
that will generate a
notification whenever

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.920
that event is triggered.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.530
The notification can optionally
contain documentation

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.020
and metadata to provide
additional information

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.140
to the person
receiving the alert.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.180
Alert notifications can be
sent to a variety of channels,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.440
including email, SMS,
Slack, PagerDuty, Webhooks,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.460
Pub/Sub, and more.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.850
So to create an
Alert Policy, you

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.410
click the Create
Alert Policy button,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.479
and you Add a Condition.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:13.680
So as you can see here,
the first option you have

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.205
is to select your
resource or metric type.

00:02:16.205 --> 00:02:18.450
And so that begs the question,
what types of metrics

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.270
are available to alert on?

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.180
All Google Cloud services
emit a variety of metrics.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.520
That includes Compute Engine,
GKE, Cloud SQL, pretty much

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.760
all of the services.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.970
So there's a good selection
of metrics to choose from.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.720
There are also different
types of metrics,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.770
for example, custom metrics.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.820
These are application-specific
metrics that you define

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.400
and collect to capture
information that the built-in

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.990
GCP metrics cannot.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.390
So these can be engineering
or business metrics,

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.000
for example, account
of returning,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.280
user checkouts in an
e-commerce system.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.770
You can also collect
log-based metrics,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.960
which are based on the
content of log entries,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.760
for example, the
number of log entries

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.690
that contain a
particular message.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.710
Or you can even extrapolate
and see your other information

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.630
reported in your log entries.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.280
Cloud Monitoring
and Logging also

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.080
have agents available
that allow you

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.180
to collect a range of
metrics that are not

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.520
covered by GCP core metrics.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.530
That includes, for
example, memory usage,

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.830
disk allocation, et cetera.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.140
And lastly, you can collect
uptime metrics, which

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:26.360
are produced by uptime checks,
which send probes from six

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.270
different regions worldwide
to monitor your web pages,

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.730
your APIs, and your services.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.330
And they emit metrics
for liveliness, latency,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.290
and several other features
that you can alert on.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:43.430
We also support a broad range of
metrics, both for other clouds,

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.730
and for the most popular open-
and closed-source applications.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.130
So no matter what
applications you're running

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.380
or what infrastructure
you're on,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.050
Google Cloud Monitoring is
able to support you, both,

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.152
for monitoring and alerting.

00:03:57.152 --> 00:03:58.610
So we're going to
talk a little bit

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.110
about the metric
and resource model.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.318
And I'm going to
hand it over to Syed.

00:04:02.318 --> 00:04:03.860
SYED ASHRAFULLA: In
Cloud Monitoring,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.880
every time series is identified
by two things, the metric

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.140
and the resource.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.950
The resource is what you
are trying to monitor.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:14.790
It can be a virtual machine.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.329
It can be a load balancer.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:20.339
It can even be something
as abstract as a service

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.370
endpoint or URL.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.040
The metric is how
you're trying to make

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.410
that measurement, something
like request count,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:30.740
or latency, or something
bespoke to you, like number

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.250
of customers that are
using a certain resource.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.000
From that ID, you will
get a set of time series.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.220
That's what's shown here at top.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.680
This is the raw data
for CPU utilization

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.860
across multiple projects and
instances in different regions.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.280
The first thing to do
in Cloud Monitoring

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.810
is to align the data.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.810
This way, all the data
is regularly sampled

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.650
and is a summarization of the
raw data point in each window.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.460
In this case, we've chosen
to do Align Next Older, which

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.920
means, for each window, we
take the most recent point

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.540
and move it to 1, 2, 3, 4,
or other minute boundaries.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.960
Now, every time series is a
line, so we can aggregate them.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.820
We can aggregate all of
them into one time series.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.360
Now we have the
mean CPU utilization

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.070
across our entire fleet.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:22.670
Often, we also want to
break this mean utilization,

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.400
as a summary, down, in
this case by region.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.830
And now we have regional
mean utilization.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.370
Both alignment and
group [? buys ?]

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.030
are going to be
very important when

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.920
you want to configure the
Alert Policy in the next steps.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.200
To talk about
alignment, it's best

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.710
to describe how an Alert
Policy is actually evaluated.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.320
So here is a raw set of data.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.307
We picked an alignment
period of five minutes.

00:05:47.307 --> 00:05:48.140
What does that mean?

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.970
It means take all the points
in the five-minute window.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.510
In this case, calculate
the mean, which is 2.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.190
Then, alerting kicks in.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.950
We set the threshold,
which is 13.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:01.340
And then, we decide a duration,
which is three minutes.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.350
The way the threshold
works is, it says,

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:07.590
are these points indicative
of the condition being active?

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.680
Then, the duration
says, is the condition

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.150
active for three minutes?

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.520
In this case, none of
the points indicate

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.960
that the condition is active.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.870
So the alert doesn't fire.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.110
Alerting is real-time.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.610
So then, we move
to the next minute.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.890
And we say, over this
five-minute window,

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.150
what does the query return?

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:25.520
The query returns a
different value, still

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.300
below the threshold.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.960
And when it returns
the different value,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.600
then we apply the duration
window a second time.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.300
And we say, none of the points
are above the threshold.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.800
The alert doesn't fire.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.640
We repeat again and
again, until there

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.390
is a point above the threshold.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.450
The alert, however,
still doesn't fire.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:48.510
Duration is telling you,
over the whole 3 minutes,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.980
are all the points, all the
queries above the threshold?

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.320
It's not the case until here.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.900
Now all three points
are above the threshold,

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:57.950
and the alert fires.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.150
Your phone goes off.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.760
You get an email, or any one
of your other notification

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.070
channels that are
configured, are fired.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:05.700
The alert we'll
continue to be active.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:06.700
It's a real-time system.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:10.160
We're continuing to check
that the alert is active

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.500
until the alert finally
stops being active.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.720
The first time we get
a query point that's

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.930
below the threshold, the alert
is now considered inactive

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.190
by Cloud Monitoring.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.580
And you will get a
resolution notification

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.440
on any of your
notification channels.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:28.910
And that is the end of how
an Alert Policy is evaluated.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:32.480
From here, I'll now send it to
Rory to actually demonstrate

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.203
some use cases of alerting
and Cloud Monitoring.

00:07:37.203 --> 00:07:38.870
RORY PETTY: So the
first demo that we're

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:42.350
going to present is creating
an alert query, where we'll

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:45.290
get into a lot of the concepts
that Syed just mentioned,

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:49.010
in terms of alignment,
filtering, aggregation, et

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:49.510
cetera.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:50.177
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:07:50.177 --> 00:07:54.080
- We'll start by looking at
an example Kubernetes cluster.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.950
Here we see the demo cluster
with five different nodes

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:01.475
and several different
microservices running on pods.

00:08:01.475 --> 00:08:02.850
Now, normally, we
would recommend

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.120
using Service Monitoring
to create SLO-based alerts

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.520
for monitoring your
microservices running

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.810
on Kubernetes in production.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.600
However, today,
we're going to do

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.820
just the simple
infrastructure-based alert

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.270
to illustrate some of the
concepts behind alerting.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.740
So we'll go into
Cloud Monitoring.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.290
We select Alerting
here in the Left Nav.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.870
And let's click Create Policy.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.740
So let's start by creating
an Alert Policy that

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:35.470
will alert us if the GKE node
CPU utilization goes too high.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.940
We click Add a Condition.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.950
And we'll search for the
Kubernetes node resource type.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:47.415
And then let's select CPU
Allocatable Utilization.

00:08:47.415 --> 00:08:49.790
Once we select that, we see
a couple of different options

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.550
appear here in the left column.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.230
Filtering lets us
reduce the amount

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:58.118
of data returned for a metric
by specifying filter criteria.

00:08:58.118 --> 00:08:59.660
This removes some
data from the chart

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.940
by excluding time series that
don't meet that criteria.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.300
For example, we see
here, we have time series

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.980
for five different nodes.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.500
But if we click on
Filter, we can filter down

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:13.310
those available time series
by project ID, location,

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.460
node name, a bunch
of different options.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.510
In this case, let's
select node name.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:21.670
And we could say, that
we wanted to filter

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:25.570
by all of the nodes that start
with a certain string prefix,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.400
like GKE/demo.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.650
Or we could say,
very specifically,

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:34.270
we want to filter and show only
this specific instance ID name.

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:36.397
As you can see, once
we selected the filter,

00:09:36.397 --> 00:09:37.980
now we only have a
single time series.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.680
Group By works by identifying
sets of time series

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.060
that all meet some criterion and
then combining or aggregating

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.980
the members of
those sets together.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.890
Grouping is done on the basis
of resource and metric labels.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:56.740
For example here,
we go in, and we

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:02.050
can group by project IDs,
location, cluster names,

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:04.990
user metadata labels,
system metadata,

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.070
lots of available options.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.280
So in this particular
case, let's go ahead

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.140
and group by location.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.290
We see here, all
of our time series

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:19.310
were replaced by a single time
series in a single location, US

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.143
Central1-a.

00:10:21.143 --> 00:10:23.310
And another way that you
can kind of understand this

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.850
is, if we removed the Group
By, and the aggregator--

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.840
let's go back here and
look at our Legend.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.520
If you notice here, the legend
contains different columns

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.902
or label keys.

00:10:34.902 --> 00:10:36.360
So if we go ahead
and select these,

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.850
we see all these time series
have these different label

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:39.390
values--

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.480
location, cluster name,
project ID, et cetera.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.170
So again, Group By
lets us reduce down

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.270
to a single time series--

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.250
or pardon me-- it
lets us reduce down

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.047
to time series grouped
by a single label value,

00:10:54.047 --> 00:10:55.005
in this case, location.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.370
Aggregation lets us
combine time series

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.580
by using common functions,
sometimes called reducers.

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:06.100
The available reducing
functions depend on the type

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:07.990
of values the metric captures.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.580
But they commonly
include choices

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.900
like mean, max, min, standard
deviation, assorted percentile

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:17.560
values, other kind of
statistical operations.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:20.380
When used with grouping,
the reducing functions

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:23.230
are applied across all of
the selected time series,

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:27.250
combining them to a single
time series consisting of mean,

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:30.820
sum, or other measure, as is
calculated across all the time

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:31.640
series.

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.250
So when used with
grouping, again,

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:37.550
this function is applied to the
time series within each group.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.150
So again, just to see
how that would work,

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:44.590
if we select Mean here, we're
taking the mean or the average

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:48.310
across those five time series
produced by all of our nodes.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:51.310
And here, we see a
single time series there.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.540
Or perhaps, we want to look
at the 99th percentile, which

00:11:55.540 --> 00:12:00.580
is useful for latency metrics
or many other kinds of metrics.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.880
Now, we'll go into
Advanced Options here.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:09.050
And if you remember from
Syed's demonstration earlier,

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:12.530
one of the most important
fields to set for Alert Policies

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:14.270
is the alignment period.

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:17.990
So take for example, an
alert that you specify

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:22.310
where you want to say, alert me
if my CPU utilization is over

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:24.710
90% for 10 minutes.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.170
If you remember
from Syed's talk,

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.800
the for-10-minutes part is
actually the alignment period

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:31.880
that we want to set.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.450
What this does is it says,
look at the last 10 minutes,

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.580
and take all the raw points
that have been written

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.470
for a given time series.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:42.230
And then, apply an aligner,
which, in this case,

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.660
it defaults to a mean aligner.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.520
So effectively, take
all those points,

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.730
average them, and produce a
single point for the last 10

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:53.100
minutes that will be used
as part of our evaluation.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.900
So we'll go here, and we'll
set just an arbitrary threshold

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.575
so that we can talk to
this a little bit better.

00:12:59.575 --> 00:13:00.950
But now that we
have a threshold,

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:05.690
we can see our points have been
aligned using the mean aligner

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.400
over the last 10 minutes.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:10.350
And here is how the time
series are evaluating.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:13.460
We have three of them that are
below the threshold and then

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.320
two that are above.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.830
So now, if we scroll
down to Configuration,

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.430
we see the next
important option,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.310
which is, this
alert condition will

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:24.800
trigger if, the
default option is,

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.500
if any time series violates--

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.330
so if we scroll up
here, we can see

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:33.320
that, because there are
two above the threshold,

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:35.730
this would go ahead and trigger.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.840
There are also options for
percent of times series.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.510
You could say, I want
50% of my time series

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:44.840
to be above or below the
threshold, 75%, whatever

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.200
you're interested in.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.580
You can pick an exact number,
like two of my time series,

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:50.990
three of my time series.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.530
Or you can say, all
of them have to be

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:59.510
above or below the threshold
for the condition to trigger.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.130
Now, for the
condition, we can say

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:04.990
that we want these
time series to be

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.940
above or below the threshold.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.400
We can say that we want
them to be changing

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:10.880
by a certain percentage.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.870
So perhaps, in a
dynamic environment,

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.870
I want to be alerted
if my latency increases

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:20.590
by 90% or 100%,
something like that

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:23.890
or, if, in a steady
state environment,

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:26.350
if something decreases
by a certain percentage

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:28.040
in a short amount of time.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.550
Lastly, there's also an
option for absence alerts.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:34.250
But we'll talk about
that in another demo.

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:37.040
Lastly, this "for"
option, refers

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.470
to the duration setting
that Syed also spoke

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.230
to you in his demonstration.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:44.750
For most people, for
threshold-based alerts,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:48.350
we recommend setting the value
here to the most recent value

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.840
such that, when
the time series go

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:54.410
above or below the threshold,
you are alerted immediately.

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:57.740
Now, if you'd like to use
duration or a duration value

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:00.650
to reduce false positives,
you can do that.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:03.870
For example, we could
say that, the time

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:08.390
series must cross the
threshold and must

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.100
remain above the threshold
or remain in violation

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.725
for a three-minute
period for the alert

00:15:12.725 --> 00:15:17.070
to trigger, which will,
again, reduce false positives,

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.310
but also has the risk
of making your alert be

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.520
less likely to fire based
on the shape of your data.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.020
And we'll have a
demonstration later

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:26.370
that illustrates that point.

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:30.010
So now let's go ahead
and save our condition.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:32.380
We'll also add a
notification channel so

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:36.460
that, when our alert fires,
we can be notified on email,

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:38.140
Slack, PagerDuty, et cetera.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:39.850
So we'll go ahead
and say that we

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:44.540
want an email to be sent to
you [? foodexample.com. ?]

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.330
And lastly, we'll put in
some documentation that

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:49.250
explains to the
person on call how

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:52.800
they might attempt to remediate
the issue caused by the alert.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.700
So we might say that they
should check the database logs.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:58.610
They should check the amount
of allocatable CPU quota

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:02.480
for the pods, et cetera.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:06.270
Now we'll go ahead
and save this policy.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.940
Now we see a graph here,
again, of our time series.

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.300
We also see
incidents that will--

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.400
this is where, once the
alert starts firing,

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.630
incidents will appear.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.510
Now we can go ahead,
to see what that

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:20.990
looks like, we can go into our
list of existing incidents.

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:22.070
We'll select one of them.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.340
And here, we see in
the message field,

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:33.790
it tells us exactly what
resource this particular GKE

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:36.280
node caused the
alert to trigger,

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:40.210
based on a certain time series
value over our threshold.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.910
We can see the graph
here of all of our nodes,

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:46.900
so that we can easily
compare the node in violation

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.750
to the performance
of the other nodes.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.930
And we see some labels here,
that help us, again, zero down

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:56.410
into and figure out
what the exact resources

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:57.850
that's causing our issue.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:01.270
Lastly, we can also link out
directly to logs and other ops

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:04.287
management tools, to
help us figure out

00:17:04.287 --> 00:17:05.829
what the root cause
of the problem is

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:07.026
and to remediate the issue.

00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:07.609
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:09.192
RORY PETTY: For our
second demo, we're

00:17:09.192 --> 00:17:11.020
going to talk about
absence alerts

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.863
and how they differ slightly
from threshold-based alerts.

00:17:13.863 --> 00:17:14.530
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:18.430
- To illustrate the concepts
behind absence alerts,

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:21.490
let's go ahead and select
a log-based metric that we

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:25.510
created from one of
our App Engine apps.

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:29.250
And in this example,
the App Engine app

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:31.710
was spun up at
this point in time.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.820
It started to write logs.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:36.630
And then, we disabled
the app, at which point,

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.430
it stopped writing logs.

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:41.250
As you can see
here, this graph is

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:44.420
expressed as a rate,
which is why it's spiky.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:47.460
It's how frequently the
log is being written.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.590
In our case, for
an absence alert,

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.750
we really don't
care about the rate.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:55.170
We want to just see the count
or the number of time series

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:58.150
points that were written
for the log-based metric.

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:02.580
So in this case, we will apply
a count aligner, which gives us

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:04.020
a nice, smooth line here.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.390
Again, we have kind
of a step function.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:10.410
We see missing data
or absent data here.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:12.360
Here is metric data.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:17.440
And then, it goes absent,
again, right here.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.600
- In order to create
an absence alert

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:24.410
for this metric, what you want
to do is focus on the bottom.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:26.530
You want to say, instead
of condition is above,

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:28.570
you say is absent.

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:30.460
Then, the threshold
will go away.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.920
And underneath the "for,"
you have the amount

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:36.280
of minutes that you actually
worry about this metric being

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.530
absent before your
phone goes off

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:41.590
or before you get another
type of communication.

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:44.770
In this case, we'll
choose 10 minutes.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:48.460
And the last thing here
is clicking, again,

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:51.040
on Show Advanced Options.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:54.610
You'll note that, if you want
to aggregate-- so for example,

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:59.110
you don't care that this
logs-based metric is

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:02.420
absent for any
specific custom label,

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:05.650
but you care that the
metric doesn't exist at all.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:08.050
You may want to
pick an aggregator.

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:13.030
When that happens, you will need
to pick an aligner like count.

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:15.405
For alignment period, we
suggest just picking one minute.

00:19:15.405 --> 00:19:17.155
That way you don't
have to think about it.

00:19:17.155 --> 00:19:19.160
And you can focus on
the important parameter,

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.616
which is the amount of time
that the metric is absent.

00:19:22.616 --> 00:19:24.960
[END PLAYBACK]

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:26.640
RORY PETTY: In our
third demo, we'll

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.730
discuss how alignment
period and duration interact

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:32.640
with each other and how
it affects whether or not

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.882
your alerts fire reliably.

00:19:34.882 --> 00:19:36.360
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:39.240
- To further illustrate the
differences between alignment

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.300
period and duration,
let's look at an example

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.150
here, where we're starting
to see a real spiky

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:47.370
increase in network traffic.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:51.710
We've selected the received
bytes metric for our GCE, VM

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:52.560
instance.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.990
We see over here, our
received byte count,

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:58.290
which is spiking up
and down over time.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:01.410
We've also filtered
this down to the traffic

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:05.550
sent to a very specific
VM, the demo VM.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:08.190
And so we've gone
down here and said

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:11.850
we want to be alerted
if the time series

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:15.520
values are above this threshold
for an amount of time.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:17.400
Let's say, we want
to be alerted if it's

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:20.560
above the threshold
for 10 minutes.

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:23.200
Now, naively, this
might make sense.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:26.160
But will this alert
fire every time

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.100
the line here on the graph
crosses the threshold?

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:31.930
- And the answer is no.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:33.940
Based on the
demonstration earlier,

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:38.170
we know that we care about
the 10-minute summary of bytes

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:39.190
received.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:42.610
To do that, we should not change
this field in the "for" field.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:45.010
Instead, we should go
to Advanced Options,

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.490
pick an alignment
period of 10 minutes,

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.560
and then, pick a
statistic that we like.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.540
In this case, right over 10
minutes sounds reasonable.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:57.850
This is the average
bytes received per second

00:20:57.850 --> 00:20:59.170
over every 10-minute window.

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.540
You could see that the
curve is smoother, better

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:05.740
representing the fact that you
care about a 10-minute average.

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:09.220
After this, for the "for"
field, which wasn't 10 minutes,

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:12.190
the best thing to do is to
set this to most recent value.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:15.550
That way, every time this smooth
line goes above the threshold,

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:16.840
your alert will fire.

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:18.460
And your alert will not flat.

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:20.410
Because remember, you
have a smooth curve.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:22.360
It's going to take some
effort for that curve

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:23.170
to come back down.

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:31.570
- So now that we have correctly
set the alignment period

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:36.403
and duration values, let's go
ahead and save this condition.

00:21:36.403 --> 00:21:38.320
And one other thing that
we want to illustrate

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:41.740
is the ability to add multiple
conditions to an alert policy.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.200
When we want to detect a
ping storm is happening,

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:46.950
we're interested not only
in the received bytes

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.050
for one VM for our demo VM.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:53.110
But we're actually interested
in the behavior of another VM.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:56.860
So let's go ahead and
look at our GCE instances.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:05.040
And in this case, we're
interested in CPU utilization

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:10.710
for a different VM, this one
by the name of Instance 1.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.460
So now we see that, now
that we filtered down

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.620
to this particular
instance, we see here

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:22.780
that Instance 1
CPU utilization has

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:27.280
been pegged at 100% for
quite some time now.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:31.740
So we might want to say that,
if our CPU utilization is

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:38.380
over 90% for a 10-minute
alignment period,

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:42.820
then alert us when the
most recent value crosses

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:45.140
the threshold.

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:46.720
We'll go ahead and
click Add there.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:49.180
You see now that we've
added two conditions.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.520
We have another
option that the policy

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:56.180
will trigger when it defaults
to any condition is met.

00:22:56.180 --> 00:22:59.510
So if either of these
conditions trigger,

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:01.180
you'll receive an alert.

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:04.610
You also have the option to
say, if all conditions are met.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:06.610
So both would have
to-- both conditions

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:07.870
would have to be true.

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:10.090
And then lastly, in
our case, we will

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:14.160
select All Conditions are Met
Even for Different Resources

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.620
for each condition.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:18.550
So if you remember, in our
case, the first condition

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:23.140
was filtered down to
the instance ID demo.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:26.170
And the second condition
was filtered or predicated

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:28.960
on the instance ID Instance 1.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:33.070
So this has been an example
that not only illustrates

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:36.340
both the interrelationship
between alignment and duration,

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:39.460
but also how you would
combine multiple conditions

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:40.510
within your Alert Policy.

00:23:40.510 --> 00:23:41.320
[END PLAYBACK]

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:43.195
SYED ASHRAFULLA: And
that is it for our intro

00:23:43.195 --> 00:23:45.205
to alerting and
cloud monitoring.

00:23:45.205 --> 00:23:46.580
RORY PETTY: Thanks
for listening.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.700
And have a nice day.

